event: response.reasoning_summary_text.done
data: {"sequence_number":51,"type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done","item_id":"rs_d96e4fac-4c3f-9010-af72-2bcf5ac0183b","output_index":0,"summary_index":0,"text":"The question is: \"If a magic mirror shows your future self, but only if you ask a question it cannot answer truthfully, what question do you ask to see your future, and what would the mirror reveal ab...Ask the mirror: \"Will you show me my future self in response to this question?\"\n\nThe mirror cannot answer this truthfully because:\n\n- If it says \"yes,\" that would mean it will show your future self, but then it would have answered the question truthfully by saying \"yes\" instead of showing you the future, which contradicts the showing.\n- If it says \"no,\" that would mean it will not show you the future, but then it would have answered truthfully by saying \"no\" instead of showing, which again contradicts the rule that it only shows when it can't answer truthfully.\n\nSo, it can't give a truthful answer without contradiction — it must show your future self instead.\n\nWhen it shows your future self, the mirror reveals that your future self is exactly the person who would ask this same paradoxical question to the mirror, confirming the loop: the future you is the one who posed the question that forced the showing, making the revelation self-referential and 100% accurate about the mirror's behavior."}

